<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Troubles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1567–<a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Council of Blood</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of Troubles was a special tribunal set up in 1567 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> for Philip II, to punish those involved in the iconoclastic riots and resistance of 1566. Called the Council of Blood by its victims, it condemned more than a thousand people to death, among them the counts of Egmont and Horn, and confiscated the property of many more. Its terror helped drive the Netherlands into open revolt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-troubles.jpg" alt="Council of Troubles" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Frisius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bor-Nederlantsche-oorloghen_MGG_1258.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the last years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-parma">Margaret of Parma</a>'s regency, the discontent of the high nobility, anger at the persecution of heretics and the new dioceses, and the famine of 1565 shook the regime. A League of Nobles petitioned against the persecution, and in the summer of 1566 Calvinists attacked Catholic churches. Philip II sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> with Spanish troops, and his first measures so offended the regent that she resigned in early September 1567. The council he created on 9 September was presented as advisory, but it set aside the older councils and ignored privileges such as Brabant's Joyous Entry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Alba presided, with Charles de Berlaymont and Philippe de Noircarmes as vice-presidents and jurists from the provinces such as Jacob Hessels as members, but the most influential were the Spaniards Juan de Vargas and Luis del Río, apparently the only ones allowed to vote. As the cases grew into thousands, Alba set up two criminal and two civil chambers in 1569. Commissioners hunted heretics in the provinces, trials were conducted wholly in writing, and the usual charge was high treason, whose content the councillors shaped to the needs of the moment; many contemporaries saw the proceedings as arbitrary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamoral-count-of-egmont">Lamoral, Count of Egmont</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-de-montmorency-count-of-horn">Philip de Montmorency, Count of Horn</a>, were denied trial by their peers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-the-golden-fleece">Order of the Golden Fleece</a> and executed on 5 June 1568, four days after eighteen lesser nobles were put to death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. According to Jonathan Israel, almost nine thousand people were convicted of heresy or treason, most of them in absentia, and lived in exile with their property confiscated; the exodus has been estimated at 60,000 people. The confiscations brought in half a million ducats a year, according to a letter of 1572.</p><p>Luis de Zúñiga y Requesens abolished the council on 7 June 1574 in return for a subsidy from the States-General, but as it went unpaid the council sat on without pronouncing further death sentences. On 4 September 1576 revolutionary bands under Jacques de Glymes arrested the Council of State in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, which ended the tribunal. Hessels was hanged by the rebel government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a>, while Vargas and Jeronimo de Roda escaped.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Troubles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Opposition to heavy taxation, centralisation and the persecution of Protestants hardened after the Duke of Alba arrived with a Spanish army in 1567 and set up the <b>Council of Troubles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
